The `strptime()` function parses a string according to the given
`strptime(3)` format string and returns the result as broken-down date
and time dictionary, suitable for `timelocal()` and `timegm()`.

Fields not covered by the format default to 1900-01-01 00:00:00, `isdst`
defaults to `-1` and `wday` and `yday` are computed from the parsed date.

Returns `null` if an argument is not a string or if the input does not
entirely match the format.

-- Testcase --
{%
	// full date and time
	printf("%.J\n", strptime("2022-03-22 13:51:42", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"));

	// time only, date defaults to 1900-01-01
	printf("%.J\n", strptime("13:51", "%H:%M"));

	printf("%.J\n", [
		// round trips with timegm(), timelocal() and strftime()
		timegm(strptime("22/Mar/2022:12:51:42", "%d/%b/%Y:%H:%M:%S")),
		timelocal(strptime("2022-03-22 13:51:42", "%F %T")),
		timelocal(strptime("2022-07-10 13:45:02", "%F %T")),
		strftime("%F %T", strptime("2022-03-22T13:51:42", "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S")),

		// leading and trailing whitespace is accepted
		strptime("  2022-03-22  ", " %Y-%m-%d").yday,

		// mismatching or incomplete input
		strptime("2022-03-22", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"),
		strptime("2022-03-22 junk", "%Y-%m-%d"),
		strptime("2022-13-01", "%Y-%m-%d"),
		strptime("", "%Y"),

		// invalid arguments
		strptime(2022, "%Y"),
		strptime("2022", null)
	]);
%}
-- End --

-- Vars --
TZ=CET-1CEST,M3.5.0/2,M10.5.0/3
-- End --

